<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

*You are here: the acts are recorded (15) and counted (16); now the desk learns to judge them.*

Refund policy: refunds above 500 need a recorded manager approval; a refund on a ticket with no known customer tier cannot be judged. You could write that as an `if` in a change. Then it is code, cannot be shown to an auditor, cannot be tailored per tenant, and "we do not know the tier" quietly becomes "the tier is not gold".

`ash_rules` compiles rules into an **immutable, content-hashed bundle**. Bundles in, verdicts out. No storage, no I/O, no side effects. Projecting results into state is someone else's job (lesson 18).

> **Missing data is never a pass, and never silently a violation.**

<div class="rounded-xl p-4 my-4" style="background-color: #fdf6ec; border-left: 4px solid #c2410c; color: #7c2d12;">
  <p><i class="ri-fire-fill"></i> <strong>Soot was here.</strong> I have been asked about every tier nobody recorded. An ember does not guess, and neither does the bundle — absence is an answer, and it is spelled <code>:unknown</code>.</p>
</div>

## Facts and their absence

Facts are `{subject, predicate, value}` triples. Every fact declares what its absence means:

| `missing:` | Absence means | Reported? |
|---|---|---|
| `false` (default) | the value `false` | yes, in `missing_facts` |
| `:unknown` | every rule probing it is `:unknown` | yes |
| `:no_fact` | an expected state, behaves like `false` | no |

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.RefundRules do
  use AshRules

  fact_schema do
    fact :amount_over_limit, :boolean
    fact :manager_approved, :boolean
    fact :customer_tier, :atom, one_of: [:standard, :gold], missing: :unknown
  end

  rule "large refunds need manager approval",
    id: "REF-1",
    severity: :high do
    when_requires has(:refund, :amount_over_limit, true)
    fails_when neg(:refund, :manager_approved, true)
    outcome :noncompliant, gap: "refund.manager_approval"
  end
end
```

Every rule has an `id`, a `severity`, and, because it can fire `:noncompliant`, a `gap`: the finding is filed under something, so it can be waived, tracked and aggregated. Leave any of those out and it does not compile. Try it.

## Evaluate

```elixir
{:ok, result} =
  AshRules.evaluate(Tutorial.RefundRules, [
    {:refund, :amount_over_limit, true},
    {:refund, :manager_approved, false}
  ])

{result.overall, AshRules.Result.findings(result) |> Enum.map(& &1.gap)}
```

Read `overall` first. The outcome lattice is `compliant`, `noncompliant`, `unknown`, `error`. `:unknown` and `:error` never collapse to `:compliant`.

One shape worth knowing before you read `findings/1` output: it lists every rule whose outcome is **not** `compliant` or `not_applicable` — so a rule blocked on missing data shows up with its gap as a *pending* finding, not a filed violation. `overall` is the verdict; `findings/1` is the worklist, and an `:unknown` entry means "resolve this before you can judge", which is precisely what you want on a compliance dashboard.

## Exercise: make "unknown" bite

Add a rule `REF-2`, severity `:medium`, gap `"refund.tier_check"`: refunds over the limit for customers who are not `:gold` are noncompliant. Then evaluate with `amount_over_limit: true`, `manager_approved: true`, and **no** `customer_tier` fact — the other facts compliant, so the only open question is the tier — and observe `overall`.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  rule "over-limit refunds are for gold customers",
    id: "REF-2",
    severity: :medium do
    when_requires has(:refund, :amount_over_limit, true)
    fails_when neg(:refund, :customer_tier, :gold)
    outcome :noncompliant, gap: "refund.tier_check"
  end
  ```

  With the tier missing, `REF-2` is `:unknown` and so is `overall` (`REF-1` is compliant, so nothing drags the verdict to `:noncompliant` — evaluate once more with `manager_approved: false` and watch `:noncompliant` win the lattice). `findings/1` lists the tier gap: pending, not filed. If you had left `missing:` at its default, a missing tier would read as `false`, `neg(... :gold)` would hold, and you would file a violation against a customer whose tier you simply never loaded.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** `overall: :unknown` with the tier gap pending — absence refused to become either a pass or a violation.

## Gate on the triple

A host gating an action on a verdict refuses when `overall in [:noncompliant, :unknown, :error]`. Treat the three as one unit. A guard that blocks `:noncompliant` but passes `:unknown` reinvents the bug the lattice exists to prevent.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# a validation on Refund.approve, in the ash_enterprise app
validate fn changeset, _ctx ->
  facts = AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundFacts.for(changeset)
  {:ok, r} = AshRules.evaluate(Tutorial.RefundRules, facts)

  if AshRules.Outcome.blocking?(r.overall),
    do: {:error, field: :status, message: "refund rules: #{r.overall}"},
    else: :ok
end
```

The rule set decides compliance; the **action** enforces. Same separation you will see for decisions (19) and processes (21).

## Traps worth knowing

* **Strict equality.** `80` does not match `80.0`. Declare the type the producer writes.
* **Subjects cross the wire as strings.** A bundle that went through `AshRules.Ir.encode!/1` and `decode/1` (anything stored by a control plane) probes `"refund"`, not `:refund`. Emit facts in the post-compile spelling.
* **The hash is the identity.** `Tutorial.RefundRules.__bundle__().content_hash` is stable across declaration order and changes whenever a rule changes. That is what lesson 18 pins evaluations to.
* **Debugging:** `mix ash_agent.rules --facts '[...]'` evaluates from the shell without booting the app (lesson 14).

